# Placeholder Caddyfile — task 2.2.
#
# This file exists only to complete one Let's Encrypt issuance ahead of the real
# deployment (ADR-009, mitigation 2). A site address plus a `respond` directive is all
# the ACME HTTP-01 challenge needs: Caddy answers the challenge itself, so no
# application, no PHP and no static files have to exist yet.
#
# It is committed rather than thrown away because task 13.2's production Caddyfile
# evolves from it. Starting from a file that has already completed an issuance on this
# host is cheaper than writing a fresh one and discovering on submission day which of
# the two variables changed.
#
# This file is a committed *template*. Copy it to `Caddyfile.local` and edit the copy,
# never this file; `compose.placeholder.yaml` mounts the copy, and keeping this one
# pristine is what makes the `git pull` in task 13.3 a clean fast-forward.
#
# Replace the site address in the copy with your own address before starting the stack.
# The placeholder is left in angle brackets on purpose: Caddy refuses to adapt this config
# at all while it is unedited, failing with
#   subject does not qualify for certificate: '<elastic-ip-dashed>.sslip.io'
# before any ACME request is made. A plausible-looking placeholder would instead be
# accepted and would spend a failed authorization against the shared sslip.io
# rate-limit bucket.
#
# Use the dashed form of the address — 203-0-113-5.sslip.io rather than
# 203.0.113.5.sslip.io. Both resolve to the same address, and the dashed form cannot be
# misread as a subdomain of a numeric label by anything downstream that parses labels.
#
# nip.io fallback (ADR-009, mitigation 1): a refused issuance is retried by changing that
# one line in the copy to `<elastic-ip-dashed>.nip.io {`. nip.io is a separate registered
# domain and therefore a separate rate-limit bucket, so exhaustion of sslip.io's bucket
# by strangers says nothing about it.

<elastic-ip-dashed>.sslip.io {
	respond "tic-tac-toe placeholder. TLS certificate provisioned ahead of deployment." 200
}
